": ["transitive"], "examples": ["Hang those lights from the ceiling.", "to hang a door"], "quotes": [{"text": "It is impossible but that offences will come: but woe unto him, through whom they come! It were better for him that a millstone were hanged about his neck, and he cast into the sea, than that he should offend one of these little ones.", "reference": "1611, The Holy Bible, […] (King James Version), London: […] Robert Barker, […], →OCLC, Luke 17:1-2:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(transitive, law) To kill (someone) by suspension from the neck, usually as a form of execution or suicide.", "tags": ["transitive"], "examples": ["The culprits were hanged from the nearest tree."], "quotes": [{"text": "'[…] There's every Staffordshire crime-piece ever made in this cabinet, and that's unique. The Van Hoyer Museum in New York hasn't that very rare second version of Maria Marten's Red Barn over there, nor the little Frederick George Manning—he was the criminal Dickens saw hanged on the roof of the gaol in Horsemonger Lane, by the way—'", "reference": "1963, Margery Allingham, chapter 3, in The China Governess: A Mystery, London: Chatto & Windus, →OCLC:"}, {"text": "As things go from bad to worse for Putin in his maniacal, murderous attack on Ukraine, he could end up like Milosevic, or worse. The court could change its rules and hang him, the way the Allies hanged Nazi war criminals at the end of World War II.", "reference": "2022 March 10, Peter Lucas, “Lucas: Putin has blood on his hands and The Hague must make him pay”, in Boston Herald, archived from the original on 06 Aug 2022:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(intransitive, law) To be executed by suspension by one's neck from a gallows, a tree, or other raised bar, attached by a rope tied into a noose.", "tags": ["intransitive"], "examples": ["You will hang for this, my friend."], "quotes": [], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(transitive, informal) (used in maledictions) To damn.", "tags": ["informal", "transitive"], "examples": [], "quotes": [{"text": "Helena\nThat depends on how much of the factory you show me.\nDomin\nOh, hang the factory. Oh, no, no, you shall see everything, Miss Glory. Indeed you shall. Won't you sit down?", "reference": "c. 1921 (date written), Karel Čapek, translated by Paul Selver, R.U.R. (Rossum’s Universal Robots): A Fantastic Melodrama […], Garden City, N.Y.: Doubleday, Page & Company, published 1923, →OCLC, Act 1:"}, {"text": "[H]e suddenly flung down his brush on the floor, said \"Bother!\" and \"Oh blow!\" and also \"Hang spring-cleaning!\" and bolted out of the house without even waiting to put on his coat.", "reference": "1908, Kenneth Grahame, The Wind in the Willows, London: Wordsworth Classics, published 1993, page 11:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(intransitive, informal) To loiter; to hang around; to spend time idly.", "tags": ["informal", "intransitive"], "examples": ["I didn't see anything, officer. I was just hanging."], "quotes": [{"text": "He banned spearfishing wherever he could, started the first eco-moorings in the Caribbean, stopped others from coral- and shell-collecting, and had so much fun 24/7 that some unusually powerful people began to hang with him.", "reference": "2006, Scuba Diving, numbers 1-6, page 49:"}], "synonyms": ["hang out"], "antonyms": [], "translations": [{"language": {"code": "af", "name": "Afrikaans"}, "word": "rondhang"}, {"language": {"code": "nl", "name": "Dutch"}, "word": "rondhangen"}, {"language": {"code": "nl", "name": "Dutch"}, "word": "uithangen"}, {"language": {"code": "eo", "name": "Esperanto"}, "word": "umi"}, {"language": {"code": "eo", "name": "Esperanto"}, "word": "ĉei"}, {"language": {"code": "fi", "name": "Finnish"}, "word": "roikkua"}, {"language": {"code": "fi"